Unapplied AMB1 SDK extension; NOT an OTA-private mutex or hardware enablement.

Apply from the framework-realtek-amb1 package root with `patch -p1` only after
reviewing the selected SDK. The host test applies this artifact to a temporary
copy of the installed SDK source; it never changes the package or builder.

Integration gate:
* This patch is specific to the FreeRTOS SDK (not its other OS backends).
* During single-task startup, before any concurrent device-lock users, call
  device_mutex_prepare(RT_DEV_LOCK_FLASH) and require exactly 1. Allocation is
  permitted there, never in device_mutex_try_lock(). Do not free/reinitialize
  this lock for the lifetime of any SDK users. The existing private free helper
  is not made public or used here.
* try_lock returns exactly 1 after taking the SAME private SDK mutex, otherwise
  0 with no acquisition, allocation, logging, retry, or positive timeout.
  It is task-only, not ISR/timer/network callback safe. No recursive acquisition.
* A future SDK binding may map the backend callback to this primitive and release
  with device_mutex_unlock(RT_DEV_LOCK_FLASH) only after successful acquisition.
  Retain the backend's identity, boot-lifetime, owner-task, busy/nonreentry and
  exactly-once close checks; this primitive does not replace those checks or
  authenticate owner pointers. Native FreeRTOS mutex ownership is task identity.
  The adapter must enforce dedicated task context before calling this function.
* No binding is supplied: current FAL/chip-ID readers do not all cooperate.
  Audit/quiesce ROM, IRQ, DMA, mapped and library users, stage callback data away
  from flash/network dependencies, qualify actual program footprint, execution
  safety and timing, then review narrow authorization separately. All existing
  guards and production denial remain unchanged. Host mocks prove only the
  source-level same-mutex/zero-tick contract, not hardware exclusion or timing.

The public blocking API and enum remain source compatible. Initialization now
marks a mutex initialized only after allocation succeeds; startup must abort OTA
on prepare failure. Legacy void callers still have their old inability to report
allocation failure and are not made allocation-failure-safe by this extension.

--- a/component/os/os_dep/device_lock.c
+++ b/component/os/os_dep/device_lock.c
@@ -9,6 +9,8 @@
 
 #include "osdep_service.h"
 #include "device_lock.h"
+#include <FreeRTOS.h>
+#include <semphr.h>
 
 //------------------------------------------------------
 #define DEVICE_MUTEX_IS_INIT(device)	(mutex_init & (1<<device))
@@ -27,7 +29,8 @@
 		rtw_enter_critical(&lock, &irqL);
 		if(!DEVICE_MUTEX_IS_INIT(device)){
 			rtw_mutex_init(&device_mutex[device]);
-			DEVICE_MUTEX_SET_INIT(device);
+			if(device_mutex[device] != NULL)
+				DEVICE_MUTEX_SET_INIT(device);
 		}
 		rtw_exit_critical(&lock, &irqL);
 	}
@@ -48,6 +51,25 @@
 	}
 }
 
+/* Startup only: finish allocation before enabling concurrent SDK users. */
+int device_mutex_prepare(RT_DEV_LOCK_E device)
+{
+	if((unsigned int)device >= RT_DEV_LOCK_MAX)
+		return 0;
+	device_mutex_init(device);
+	return DEVICE_MUTEX_IS_INIT(device) && device_mutex[device] != NULL;
+}
+
+/* Task only, preinitialized mutex: no lazy allocation or timeout logging. */
+int device_mutex_try_lock(RT_DEV_LOCK_E device)
+{
+	if((unsigned int)device >= RT_DEV_LOCK_MAX)
+		return 0;
+	if(!DEVICE_MUTEX_IS_INIT(device) || device_mutex[device] == NULL)
+		return 0;
+	return xSemaphoreTake(device_mutex[device], 0) == pdTRUE ? 1 : 0;
+}
+
 //======================================================
 void device_mutex_lock(RT_DEV_LOCK_E device)
 {
--- a/component/os/os_dep/include/device_lock.h
+++ b/component/os/os_dep/include/device_lock.h
@@ -20,6 +20,11 @@
 	RT_DEV_LOCK_MAX = 5
 }RT_DEV_LOCK_E;
 
+/* Single-task startup allocation; exactly 1 means ready, 0 means failure. */
+int device_mutex_prepare(RT_DEV_LOCK_E device);
+/* Task-only, prepared shared mutex. Exactly 1 means acquired, else 0. */
+int device_mutex_try_lock(RT_DEV_LOCK_E device);
+
 void device_mutex_lock(RT_DEV_LOCK_E device);
 void device_mutex_unlock(RT_DEV_LOCK_E device);
 
